  "grammar_points": [
    {
      "name": "〜まま (as it is)",
      "jlpt_reference": "JLPT N3",
      "explanation": {
        "en": "How it works: Place まま (mama) after a verb (past or negative form) to express that a state remains unchanged — 'as it is' or 'while still in that condition'.\n\nExample from this song: わからないまま (wakaranai mama) = 'remaining not understanding' — from わからない (wakaranai, 'don't understand') + まま (mama, 'as is')\n\nMore examples:\n• 立つ (tatsu, 'to stand') → 立ったまま (tatta mama, 'while still standing')\n• 電気をつける (denki o tsukeru, 'turn on light') → つけたまま (tsuketa mama, 'left turned on')\n• 食べない (tabenai, 'not eat') → 食べないまま (tabenai mama, 'without eating')\n\nNote: Negative + まま = a state that hasn't changed (still not doing). Past + まま = a state left as is (did it and left it). Both imply the state persists."
      },
      "conjugation_path": "わからない (wakaranai, 'don't understand') + まま (mama, 'as is') → わからないまま (wakaranai mama, 'remaining not understanding')"
    },
    {
      "name": "〜ていく (continuing away)",
      "jlpt_reference": "JLPT N3",
      "explanation": {
        "en": "How it works: Take the て-form (te-form) and add いく (iku, 'to go'). This shows an action continuing forward into the future, or gradually moving away from the present.\n\nExample from this song: 振り払っていく (furiharatte iku) = 'keep shaking off' — from 振り払う (furiharau, 'to shake off') → 振り払って (furiharatte, te-form) + いく (iku)\n\nMore examples:\n• 消える (kieru, 'to disappear') → 消えていく (kiete iku, 'gradually disappear')\n• 強くなる (tsuyoku naru, 'become strong') → 強くなっていく (tsuyoku natte iku, 'keep getting stronger')\n• 進む (susumu, 'to advance') → 進んでいく (susunde iku, 'keep moving forward')\n\nNote: ていく = forward/away direction. てくる = toward/approaching direction. This directional pair is fundamental to understanding progressive actions in Japanese."
      },
      "conjugation_path": "振り払う (furiharau, 'to shake off') → 振り払って (furiharatte, te-form) → 振り払っていく (furiharatte iku, 'keep shaking off')"
    },
    {
      "name": "〜ば (conditional)",
      "jlpt_reference": "JLPT N4",
      "explanation": {
        "en": "How it works: For godan verbs, change the final -u to -eba. For ichidan verbs, drop る and add れば. This creates the conditional 'if' — a general or hypothetical condition.\n\nExample from this song: いれば (ireba) = 'if you are here' — from いる (iru, 'to be') → drop る, add れば → いれば. Also 触れれば (furereba) = 'if I touch' — from 触れる (fureru, 'to touch') → 触れれば\n\nMore examples:\n• 行く (iku, 'to go') → 行けば (ikeba, 'if you go')\n• 食べる (taberu, 'to eat') → 食べれば (tabereba, 'if you eat')\n• 安い (yasui, 'cheap') → 安ければ (yasukereba, 'if it's cheap')\n\nNote: The ば conditional is often used for general truths and hypotheticals. It pairs well with いい (ii): 行けばいい (ikeba ii, 'you should just go'). It's one of four Japanese conditionals (ば, たら, なら, と)."
      },
      "conjugation_path": "いる (iru, 'to be') → いれば (ireba, 'if you are here') / 触れる (fureru, 'to touch') → 触れれば (furereba, 'if I touch')"
    },
    {
      "name": "〜たい (want to)",
      "jlpt_reference": "JLPT N4",
      "explanation": {
        "en": "How it works: Take the verb masu-stem and add たい (tai). This expresses the speaker's desire to do something. It conjugates like an い-adjective.\n\nExample from this song: 感じたい (kanjitai) = 'want to feel' — from 感じる (kanjiru, 'to feel') → 感じ (kanji, masu-stem) + たい (tai)\n\nMore examples:\n• 食べる (taberu, 'to eat') → 食べたい (tabetai, 'want to eat')\n• 会う (au, 'to meet') → 会いたい (aitai, 'want to meet')\n• 知る (shiru, 'to know') → 知りたい (shiritai, 'want to know')\n\nNote: たい is for the speaker's own desires (first person). For third person, use たがっている (tagatteiru): 彼は食べたがっている (kare wa tabetagatteiru, 'he wants to eat'). Past: たかった (takatta, 'wanted to')."
      },
      "conjugation_path": "感じる (kanjiru, 'to feel') → 感じ (kanji, masu-stem) → 感じたい (kanjitai, 'want to feel')"
    },
    {
      "name": "〜ても (even if)",
      "jlpt_reference": "JLPT N4",
      "explanation": {
        "en": "How it works: Take the て-form (te-form) and add も (mo). This creates 'even if' — a concessive meaning where the result holds true regardless of the condition.\n\nExample from this song: 纏っても (matottemo) = 'even if wrapped in' — from 纏う (matou, 'to wear/wrap') → 纏って (matotte, te-form) + も (mo)\n\nMore examples:\n• 失敗する (shippai suru, 'to fail') → 失敗しても (shippai shitemo, 'even if I fail')\n• 寒い (samui, 'cold') → 寒くても (samukutemo, 'even if it's cold')\n• 雨 (ame, 'rain') → 雨でも (ame demo, 'even if it rains')\n\nNote: ても is the go-to concessive pattern in Japanese. It's one of the most common grammar points across all JLPT levels because of its versatility and frequency in both speech and writing."
      },
      "conjugation_path": "纏う (matou, 'to wrap') → 纏って (matotte, te-form) → 纏っても (matottemo, 'even if wrapped in')"
    }
  ]
